<article class="env-lemma" id="05GN">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05GN">Lemma <span data-tag="05GN">15.15.2</span>.</a> An auto-associated ring $R$ has the following property: (P) Every proper finitely generated ideal $I \subset R$ has a nonzero annihilator. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By assumption there exists a nonzero element $x \in R$ such that for every $f \in \mathfrak m$ we have $f^ n x = 0$. Say $I = (f_1, \ldots , f_ r)$. Then $x$ is in the kernel of $R \to \bigoplus R_{f_ i}$. Hence we see that there exists a nonzero $y \in R$ such that $f_ i y = 0$ for all $i$, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0565" data-tag="0565">10.24.4</a>. As $y \in \text{Ann}_ R(I)$ we win. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>